There is a unique $A\in\mathbb Q[[x]]$ such that $[x^0]A=0$, $[x^1]A=1$ and
\begin{equation}
 A=A_2+\frac{A_2^2 A_3}{A_6}.
\end{equation}
For every integer $n\ge 1$, $[x^n]A$ is a positive integer.

The solution also satisfies the dyadic expansion in A382316, FORMULA~(2):
\begin{equation}
 A(x)=\sum_{r\ge1}
 \frac{A(x^{2^r})^2 A(x^{3\cdot2^{r-1}})}{A(x^{3\cdot2^r})}.
\end{equation}

The series $A$ has radius of convergence $1$ and maps $[0,1)$ strictly
increasingly onto $[0,\infty)$. Let $\alpha$ be the unique positive solution of
\begin{equation}
 \frac{2^{-\alpha}}{1-2^{-\alpha}}+3^{-\alpha}=1.
\end{equation}
There are constants $c,C>0$ such that for all sufficiently small $t>0$,
\begin{equation}
 c t^{-\alpha}\le \log A(e^{-t})\le C t^{-\alpha}.
\end{equation}
